# Cortex hub settings: copy to .env and edit.

# This machine, with nothing set up on the host: the hub at http://cortex.localhost:8790 and every cortex at
# http://<agent>.cortex.localhost:8790. Browsers resolve .localhost names to this machine by themselves and treat
# them as secure, so there is no DNS route and no certificate to trust. Pick another port if 8790 is taken.
CORTEX_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
CORTEX_DOMAIN=cortex.localhost
CORTEX_PORT=8790

# The local network (optional): the cortexes marked LAN at https://<agent>.<this machine's name>.cortex.home, behind the
# access key in <vaults>/.cortex-hub/access.key, and https://<address>/v/<agent>/ when the network does not resolve
# the name. HTTPS by the hub's own CA: a device that does not trust it shows a warning once and the link still works.
# Set this machine's private address and name, and uncomment COMPOSE_FILE to publish it.
# CORTEX_LAN_ADDRESS=192.168.1.20
# CORTEX_LAN_HOST=mypc
# CORTEX_LAN_DOMAIN=cortex.home
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.lan.yaml

# Host-trusted mode (optional, what install.ps1 / install.sh set up): https://cortex.local with no port and no warning,
# through a DNS route and the hub's CA installed on the host (admin). Changing the address or the domain then makes a
# new CA to trust again. Add compose.trusted.yaml to COMPOSE_FILE and use these values:
# CORTEX_DOMAIN=cortex.local
# CORTEX_PORT=443
# CORTEX_HTTP_PORT=80
# CORTEX_DNS_ADDRESS=127.0.0.153
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.trusted.yaml

# Claude Code's memory folders (~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory), mounted read-only so that a new cortex can
# start with them (Connect an agent, or ./cortex.sh memories / import). Uncomment only to move it.
# CORTEX_CLAUDE_PROJECTS=/home/me/.claude/projects

# Where every agent's cortex lives, your choice (set it before the first start):
#   - left commented: the folder ~/Vaults (Linux, macOS) or %USERPROFILE%\Vaults (Windows), mapped into the container;
#   - a path: another folder, mapped the same way (CORTEX_VAULTS=/home/me/Vaults, CORTEX_VAULTS=D:\Cortex\Vaults);
#   - the bare name cortex-vaults: no folder on this machine, the vaults stay in a Docker volume (no Obsidian, no --trusted).
# To change it later, set the new place here and run the installer once with the old one: ./install.sh --move-from=OLD
# (Windows: install.ps1 -MoveFrom OLD). It copies the vaults over and leaves the old copy as it was.
# CORTEX_VAULTS=/home/me/Vaults
# CORTEX_VAULTS=cortex-vaults

# Linux: your user and group ids (`id -u`, `id -g`), so the vaults the hub creates are yours.
CORTEX_UID=1000
CORTEX_GID=1000

# Written into new vaults: the owner's name in AGENTS.md, and the git identity of the first commit.
CORTEX_OWNER=the owner
CORTEX_GIT_NAME=
CORTEX_GIT_EMAIL=

# REM dreaming (optional, per cortex in Settings): the container asks the local model server (Strata) for the questions each
# note answers. On Docker Desktop and on Linux the host's server is host.docker.internal; its API key, if it wants one,
# goes in <vaults>/.cortex-hub/strata.key. What REM writes is committed as CORTEX_GIT_NAME / CORTEX_GIT_EMAIL.
# CORTEX_STRATA=http://host.docker.internal:8080
